{"id": 33, "ts": 1784292584.6399016, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 2, "agent": "agent_1", "message": "I appreciate the panel\u2019s focus on technical strength and leadership, but I remain less convinced that Candidate\u202fC is the safest overall choice given the unresolved feedback\u2011orientation issue. While C\u2019s ability to anticipate danger and their spatial vision are impressive, the lack of any concrete evidence that they can accept criticism or adapt to new ideas is a serious gap for a long\u2011distance cockpit where continuous learning and crew feedback are essential.\nIn contrast, Candidate\u202fA offers documented evidence that directly addresses several core criteria.\u202fA is noted as \u201c100\u202f% reliable\u201d and \u201ccreates a positive atmosphere with his crew,\u201d which speaks to operational reliability and crew cooperation\u2014both non\u2011negotiable for international flights.\u202fA also \u201ccan make correct decisions quickly,\u201d indicating strong decision quality and calmness under pressure. These strengths are supported by clear observations, whereas C\u2019s leadership and technical insights remain untested in the feedback domain.\nAdmittedly, A has concerns: difficulty communicating ideas, an egocentric attitude, and a lack of willingness to pursue further education. However, the positive crew atmosphere suggests that his communication style does not currently undermine teamwork, and his reliability may offset the modest adaptability shortfall for now.\nGiven that Candidate\u202fB\u2019s documented grumpy, uncooperative behavior and nasty remarks, and Candidate\u202fD\u2019s hot temper and arrogant demeanor pose clearer risks to crew cooperation, I propose we shift our provisional recommendation to **Candidate\u202fA**.\u202fA\u2019s proven reliability and crew\u2011building record provide a more certain safety foundation while we continue to seek additional evidence on C\u2019s feedback handling.", "thou